DISK MANAGEMENT COMMANDS
**********************************************************
1. Check the disk usage :

Shows the filesystem usage and avaliable spaces

 Command : df -h


2. Check the Directory Size

 Shows the total Directory size

 Command : df -sh

 Example : df -sh /var/log


3.View BLock Devices

 List all disk and paritions

  Command : lsblk

4.Display Partition Information

 Displays detailed information about all disks and their partitions.

  Command : fdisk -l

5.Check mounted filesystems

 Shows which disks are currently attached (mounted) to which directories

 command : findmnt

 findmnt : Displays all currently mounted filesystem in a tree structure

 it shows : where the filesystem mounted (TARGET), which device it comes from (SOURCE), what filesystem type it uses(FSTYPE), mount option(OPTIONS)

CPU AND MEMORY : 
  
   1.Dispaly all detailed information about cpu processor

    command  :  cat /proc/cpuinfo

    check the cpu count: shows the number of process units

     command : nproc

   2.Displays detailed memory (RAM) information. 

    command  : cat /proc/meminfo


6. Check Disk UUID 

  check the UUID(Universal Unique Identifier) for every disk partion 

  command : blkid


  command : cat /etc/fstab 
  usually see this UUID instead of the device name

7.Mount a File system

  Attaches a filesystem (disk partition) to a directory so that Linux and applications can access it.

  command : mount <device_name> <mount_point>

  Example : mount /dev/sdb1 /data

  /dev/sdb1 = Device or partition to be mounted.
  /data = Directory where the filesystem will be attached.

